From 55d127da94c7151be51dee0ecf00a544ba6f50b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: guochao <guochao@local>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:32:27 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] LinuxBridge: setuid tap creation fallback and
 case-insensitive properties

Two issues found in end-to-end testing with a real VM:

1. TUNSETIFF to create a new TAP interface requires CAP_NET_ADMIN, which the
   VM process does not have; it failed with EPERM ('Operation not permitted')
   even though /dev/net/tun itself is world accessible.  The driver now falls
   back to the setuid helper on EPERM/EACCES: VBoxNetBrCtl gained 'create
   <ifname> <uid>' and 'destroy <ifname>' subcommands that create a persistent
   TAP owned by the calling user (uid must equal the caller's real uid, and
   the vboxtap* name restriction still applies).  The driver then attaches to
   the existing interface by name - which works for the owner without any
   capability - and immediately clears persistence again so the interface is
   removed by the kernel when the VM releases the fd (clean exit or crash).
   The helper now uses linux/if.h + linux/if_tun.h instead of glibc's
   <net/if.h> (uapi conflict) with a local SIOCGIFINDEX-based ifIndexGet().

2. Generic driver property names come from user input and CFGM compares keys
   strictly case-sensitively, so 'bridge' was rejected while 'Bridge' passed.
   The driver now validates and reads its properties case-insensitively
   (value enumeration via pfnCFGMGetFirstValue/GetValueName + RTStrICmp),
   keeping CFGM's own allocator in charge of the returned strings.

Verified end-to-end: VM boots, vboxtap0 appears enslaved to vboxbr0 in
forwarding state, dnsmasq leases 192.168.56.175 to the guest.
